  "body": "Right now, [`HIDDevice.close()` step 2](https://hid.spec.whatwg.org/#ref-for-dfn-state-7) reads:\n\n> If [this](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#this).[[[state]]](https://hid.spec.whatwg.org/#dfn-state) is either \"forgotten\" or \"forgetting\", then [reject](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#reject) promise with a \"[InvalidStateError](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#invalidstateerror)\" [DOMException](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#idl-DOMException) and return promise.\n\nI propose we should extend this to include the \"opening\" and \"closing\" states.\n\nBecause the current text doesn't include the \"opening\" state, we can end up in a racy situation like this:\n```js\nconst opening = device.open();\nawait device.close();\n```\nNow the `close()` can finish before the `open()`, and so the device can end up in an open state depending on which call wins the race. While this isn't a possibility for two `close()` calls, I think it makes sense to include the \"closing\" state in this for consistency.\n\nNote that Blink currently does this: [`HIDDevice.close()` checks for whether a device change is in progress](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/modules/hid/hid_device.cc;l=294;drc=c9c5aa46178af92bcfcd7e54dbb14341c7cef37d) (which includes being in the middle of an `open()` call) and rejects the promise if so.",
